<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext HTTP is permitted, in every build type.

    Android flipped this default in API 28: with `targetSdk = 36` the platform denies cleartext
    unless an app says otherwise, so without this file every `http://` URL fails with

        java.net.UnknownServiceException: CLEARTEXT communication to <host> not permitted

    before a socket is opened - which reads like the server is down or the route is wrong, and is
    neither.

    Why it is permitted rather than restricted to a host list: a self-hosted sync server's address is
    typed by the person running it, into the Server URL field on the account screen. It can be any
    hostname or IP on their network, and this file is compiled at build time - so there is no list to
    write. `deploy/docker-compose.yml` fronts the server with Caddy, and https is the better answer
    where TLS is available, but a plain-http server on a LAN is a supported configuration rather than
    a mistake to block. That is a deliberate product decision, not an oversight.

    What it costs, so nobody has to rediscover it: over http, a self-hoster's account password
    (`POST /v1/devices` is the one endpoint that takes it) and the device token returned by that call
    are readable by anything else on the network path. The token authorises every later sync request
    and the server can never reissue it - only its SHA-256 is stored. Anyone on an untrusted network
    should be reaching their server over https.

    `trust-anchors` is deliberately not overridden here: the platform default (system CAs, no
    user-installed certificates) still applies, so this loosens cleartext and nothing else. A
    self-hoster with a private CA needs a separate, deliberate decision - not a side effect of this
    file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
